  <!-- ================= BRONZE ================= -->
  <section id="bronze">
    <div class="hd"><h2 class="sec">Bronze. Land it raw, keep every row, run it on a schedule.</h2><span class="tier bz">bronze · usually red</span></div>
    <div class="sub">
      <p>Bronze is the only layer that is naturally cloud-shaped. It reads from outside systems, it grows without bound, and it runs at 6 a.m. whether anyone opened a laptop or not. In a warehouse you'd give it a small always-on cluster. In Lakelet it's a scheduled <code>lakelet run --select tag:bronze --burst auto</code> with a cap: a worker starts in the bucket's region, pulls the new rows, merges them into the bronze tables, and exits.</p>
      <p>The gauge calls most bronze loads red for the honest reason: they are bandwidth-bound, and a worker sitting next to your bucket has a hundred times the bandwidth of a laptop on home Wi-Fi. That is exactly the case burst exists for.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="duo">
      <Terminal title="schedule · nightly · 06:00 · runs on a worker · cap $2.00">
<pre class="softwrap"><span class="p">$</span> lakelet run --select tag:bronze --burst auto
  <span class="d">compiling 3 models · reading manifests · sizing worker</span>

  bronze.stripe_charges  <span class="d">2.1 GB new from api        ~1 min</span>
  bronze.app_events      <span class="d">4.8 GB from s3://acme-raw  ~2 min</span>
  bronze.pg_customers    <span class="d">40 MB from postgres        ~3 s</span>

  <span class="o">&gt;(°)&lt;</span> <span class="d">~~~~~~&gt;</span> M worker · 8 vCPU / 32 GB · est $0.04 · <span class="w">cap $2.00</span>

  <span class="g">✓</span> bronze.stripe_charges  <span class="d">merged 184,220 rows · 58 s</span>
  <span class="g">✓</span> bronze.app_events      <span class="d">merged 9.4M rows · 1m 51s</span>
  <span class="g">✓</span> bronze.pg_customers    <span class="d">merged 1,203 rows · 2.8 s</span>
  <span class="d">3 of 3 · 3m 04s · worker exited · commit a41f2c9</span>
  <span class="d">actual</span> <span class="w">$0.03</span> <span class="d">of $2.00 cap · on a laptop: ~7 min of download</span></pre>
      </Terminal>
      <div class="panel where">
        <h3>Bronze in practice</h3>
        <table>
          <tr><th>Question</th><th>Answer</th></tr>
          <tr><td>How does data get in?</td><td>DuckDB reads Postgres, files and HTTP APIs directly, so a bronze model can be <code>select * from postgres_scan(...)</code>. Loader tools (dlt, Airbyte, Fivetran) that write Parquet or Iceberg to the same bucket work too; Lakelet registers what they write.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Materialisation</td><td><b class="d">incremental</b>, <code>MERGE INTO</code> on the source key (DuckDB 1.5.3 Iceberg writes). Append-only tables use plain inserts.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Typical verdict</td><td><b class="n">Red</b> for anything over a few GB or anything remote; <b class="d">green</b> for small reference tables.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Where it runs</td><td><b class="c">Ephemeral worker</b> on a schedule (Team), or a laptop that happens to be open (Local).</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Partitioning</td><td><code>day(loaded_at)</code> so silver only scans what's new. The gauge reads those partitions to price the next layer.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Who reads it</td><td>Only silver models. Bronze is not for people.</td></tr>
        </table>
        <small>No connectors framework yet: bronze is bring-your-own-loader in the beta. Built-in incremental connectors are on the roadmap after the team tier.</small>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ================= SILVER ================= -->
  <section id="silver">
    <div class="hd"><h2 class="sec">Silver. Clean it on the laptop, because that is where the engineer is.</h2><span class="tier sv">silver · mostly green</span></div>
    <div class="sub">
      <p>Silver is where the work happens: casting types, deduplicating, joining reference data, applying the business's definition of a customer. It is also where engineers iterate fifty times a day, and every one of those iterations is a query. On a cloud warehouse, fifty iterations is fifty warehouse resumes. On Lakelet it's fifty local DuckDB runs against the same Iceberg files, and the bill is zero.</p>
      <p>Silver models are incremental over bronze partitions, so a daily run touches a day of data and the gauge calls it green. The exceptions are the honest ones: a backfill after a logic change, or a model that has to re-read a year of events. Those come back yellow or red with a price on them, and you choose.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="duo">
      <Terminal title="Ana's laptop · ~/acme-analytics · 16 GB · 190 Mbps">
<pre class="softwrap"><span class="p">$</span> lakelet run --select tag:silver
  silver.customers   <span class="g">●</span> Runs here <span class="d">· 60 MB · ~1 s</span>
  silver.charges     <span class="g">●</span> Runs here <span class="d">· 1 day of bronze · 210 MB · ~2 s</span>
  silver.events      <span class="g">●</span> Runs here <span class="d">· 1 day of bronze · 1.4 GB · ~14 s</span>
  <span class="g">✓</span> 3 of 3 · 17 s · 22 tests passed · <span class="w">$0.00</span>

<span class="p">$</span> <span class="d"># change the sessionisation rule, rebuild a year</span>
<span class="p">$</span> lakelet run --select silver.events --full-refresh
  silver.events      <span class="r">●</span> Needs more machine <span class="d">· 31 GB · 365 partitions</span>
                     <span class="d">· ~22 min here at 190 Mbps · burst ~1.5 min</span>
  <span class="o">&gt;(°)&lt;</span> <span class="d">~~~~~~&gt;</span> burst? L worker · 16 vCPU / 64 GB · <span class="w">cap $0.30</span>   <span class="d">[y/N]</span> <span class="w">y</span>
  <span class="g">✓</span> silver.events rebuilt · 1m 40s · actual <span class="w">$0.19</span>
  <span class="d">  22 tests passed · commit 8c1e0d2</span></pre>
      </Terminal>
      <div class="panel where">
        <h3>Silver in practice</h3>
        <table>
          <tr><th>Question</th><th>Answer</th></tr>
          <tr><td>Materialisation</td><td><b class="d">incremental</b> over bronze's <code>day(loaded_at)</code> partitions; <code>unique_key</code> dedup via <code>MERGE INTO</code>.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Typical verdict</td><td><b class="d">Green</b> for daily increments and dev iteration; <b class="y">yellow</b> for wide joins that spill; <b class="n">red</b> for full refreshes.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Where it runs</td><td><b class="d">Laptop</b> for development and small increments; the nightly run rides the same schedule as bronze.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Tests</td><td>dbt tests run after every model, locally, for free. A failed test blocks the commit to the catalog, so a bad silver never reaches gold.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Dev vs prod</td><td>Same files, same format. A dev target writes to <code>silver_ana.*</code> in the same bucket; promoting is a dbt target switch, not a migration.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Concurrency</td><td>Two laptops writing the same table commit through the catalog optimistically; the second retries on top of the first.</td></tr>
        </table>
        <small>The gauge's per-model estimate uses the Iceberg manifests of the upstream bronze tables, so it knows a one-day increment from a full refresh before it runs anything.</small>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ================= GOLD ================= -->
  <section id="gold">
    <div class="hd"><h2 class="sec">Gold. Small, fast, rebuilt whenever anyone asks.</h2><span class="tier gd">gold · green</span></div>
    <div class="sub">
      <p>Gold tables are the ones people query: daily revenue, a customer 360, DAU by country. They are aggregates of silver, which makes them small, which makes them green. On Lakelet a gold table is rebuilt on a laptop in seconds, and the same laptop answers questions against it, in SQL or in English, without a network round-trip.</p>
      <p>Gold is also the layer other tools read. Because it's Iceberg in your bucket under a REST catalog, a BI tool, a Spark job, a notebook or an agent reads gold the same way a laptop does. Nothing is exported, nothing is copied, nothing is "synced to the warehouse".</p>
    </div>
    <div class="duo">
      <Terminal title="Lakelet app · ask">
<pre class="softwrap"><span class="p">$</span> lakelet run --select tag:gold
  gold.daily_revenue    <span class="g">●</span> Runs here <span class="d">· 320 MB of silver · ~3 s</span>
  gold.customer_360     <span class="g">●</span> Runs here <span class="d">· 1.1 GB · ~9 s</span>
  gold.dau_by_country   <span class="g">●</span> Runs here <span class="d">· 2.6 GB · ~20 s</span>
  <span class="g">✓</span> 3 of 3 · 31 s · <span class="w">$0.00</span>

<span class="p">$</span> lakelet ask "revenue by plan last quarter vs the one before"
  <span class="b">select</span> plan, quarter, <span class="b">sum</span>(revenue) <span class="b">from</span> gold.daily_revenue
  <span class="b">where</span> quarter <span class="b">in</span> (current_quarter - 1, current_quarter - 2)
  <span class="b">group by</span> 1, 2
  <span class="g">●</span> Runs here <span class="d">· 18 MB · ~0.2 s</span>
  <span class="d">plan      Q2 2026     Q3 2026     Δ</span>
  starter   $184,220    $201,960    +9.6%
  team      $612,400    $688,310    +12.4%
  company   $890,000    $1,020,500  +14.7%
  <span class="d">[Save as question]  [Chart]  [Schedule]</span>

<span class="p">$</span> lakelet catalog serve --port 8181
  <span class="d"># Metabase, Tableau, Spark read gold from here</span></pre>
      </Terminal>
      <div class="panel where">
        <h3>Gold in practice</h3>
        <table>
          <tr><th>Question</th><th>Answer</th></tr>
          <tr><td>Materialisation</td><td><b class="d">table</b>, full rebuild. Gold is small enough that incremental logic isn't worth the bugs.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Typical verdict</td><td><b class="d">Green</b>. If a gold model goes red, it's usually silver's job in disguise.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Who reads it</td><td>Everyone: the Lakelet app, BI tools through <code>lakelet catalog serve</code> or the Team catalog, Spark/Trino, notebooks, agents over MCP with a daily cap.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Access</td><td>On Team, catalog roles grant gold to the company and silver/bronze to the data team; credentials are vended per table prefix.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Saved questions</td><td>A question saved in the app becomes a gold model with tests and a commit, so the mart grows from what people actually ask.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Freshness</td><td>Team alerts if gold is older than its schedule promises, or if any test on the path from bronze failed.</td></tr>
        </table>
        <small>Gold is where "your laptop is the warehouse" stops being a slogan: the tables are on the laptop, in the open format, and the questions never leave it.</small>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ================= PROJECT LAYOUT ================= -->
  <section id="project">
    <div class="eyebrow"># the project</div>
    <h2 class="sec">It's a dbt project. That's the point.</h2>
    <p class="sub">There is no Lakelet DSL. The layers are folders, the layer rules are a few lines of <code>dbt_project.yml</code>, and the only Lakelet-specific file sets burst defaults per layer. Move the project to Snowflake tomorrow and the models still run.</p>
    <div class="grid3">
      <div class="panel code">
        <div class="bar">acme-analytics/</div>
<pre>lakelet.toml
dbt_project.yml
profiles.yml         <span class="c"># laptop | team | ci</span>
AGENTS.md            <span class="c"># from lakelet init</span>
models/
  <span class="bz">bronze/</span>
    stripe_charges.sql
    app_events.sql
    pg_customers.sql
    _bronze.yml      <span class="c"># sources, freshness</span>
  <span class="sv">silver/</span>
    customers.sql
    charges.sql
    events.sql
    _silver.yml      <span class="c"># tests</span>
  <span class="gd">gold/</span>
    daily_revenue.sql
    customer_360.sql
    dau_by_country.sql
    _gold.yml        <span class="c"># exposures</span>
questions/           <span class="c"># saved from the app</span>
warehouse/           <span class="c"># local Iceberg (gitignored)</span></pre>
      </div>
      <div class="panel code">
        <div class="bar">dbt_project.yml</div>
<pre><span class="k">models</span>:
  <span class="k">acme_analytics</span>:
    <span class="bz">bronze</span>:
      <span class="k">+schema</span>: <span class="s">bronze</span>
      <span class="k">+materialized</span>: <span class="s">incremental</span>
      <span class="k">+incremental_strategy</span>: <span class="s">merge</span>
      <span class="k">+partition_by</span>: <span class="s">day(loaded_at)</span>
      <span class="k">+tags</span>: [<span class="s">bronze</span>]
    <span class="sv">silver</span>:
      <span class="k">+schema</span>: <span class="s">silver</span>
      <span class="k">+materialized</span>: <span class="s">incremental</span>
      <span class="k">+incremental_strategy</span>: <span class="s">merge</span>
      <span class="k">+tags</span>: [<span class="s">silver</span>]
    <span class="gd">gold</span>:
      <span class="k">+schema</span>: <span class="s">gold</span>
      <span class="k">+materialized</span>: <span class="s">table</span>
      <span class="k">+tags</span>: [<span class="s">gold</span>]

<span class="c"># silver/events.sql, the incremental bit</span>
<span class="c">&#123;% if is_incremental() %&#125;</span>
<span class="c">  where loaded_at &gt;
    (select max(loaded_at) from &#123;&#123; this &#125;&#125;)</span>
<span class="c">&#123;% endif %&#125;</span></pre>
      </div>
      <div class="panel code">
        <div class="bar">lakelet.toml</div>
<pre>[<span class="k">project</span>]
name      = <span class="s">"acme-analytics"</span>
warehouse = <span class="s">"s3://acme-lake"</span>

[<span class="k">catalog</span>]
mode = <span class="s">"team"</span>   <span class="c"># local|team|external</span>

[<span class="k">burst</span>]
default = <span class="s">"prompt"</span>  <span class="c"># prompt|auto|never</span>
backend = <span class="s">"aws"</span>
region  = <span class="s">"us-east-1"</span>

<span class="c"># per-layer overrides, by dbt tag</span>
[<span class="k">burst.tags</span>.<span class="bz">bronze</span>]
default = <span class="s">"auto"</span>
max_cost_per_run_usd = 2.00

[<span class="k">burst.tags</span>.<span class="sv">silver</span>]
max_cost_per_run_usd = 5.00

[<span class="k">burst.tags</span>.<span class="gd">gold</span>]
default = <span class="s">"never"</span>   <span class="c"># red gold = fix silver</span>

[<span class="k">schedules.nightly</span>]
at     = <span class="s">"06:00"</span>
select = <span class="s">"tag:bronze tag:silver tag:gold"</span>
burst  = <span class="s">"auto"</span>

[<span class="k">agents</span>]
allow = [<span class="s">"query"</span>, <span class="s">"estimate"</span>,
         <span class="s">"save_question"</span>]
cap_usd_per_day = 2.00</pre>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ================= A DAY ================= -->
  <section id="day">
    <div class="eyebrow"># one weekday</div>
    <h2 class="sec">What a Tuesday looks like.</h2>
    <p class="sub">Five-person team, 38 GB across the three layers, one scheduled run and a working day of humans and one agent. Every line is a real gauge decision; the day's cloud bill is at the bottom.</p>
    <div class="timeline">
      <div class="panel tl"><time>06:00</time><i class="r"></i><div><span class="cost">$0.06</span><h3>Nightly schedule, bronze → silver → gold</h3><p>An M worker in us-east-1 merges the day's Stripe and event data into bronze, then silver and gold run on the same worker while it's up. 9 models, 31 tests, 6 minutes, then it exits. Cap was $2.00.</p></div></div>
      <div class="panel tl"><time>09:10</time><i class="g"></i><div><span class="cost">$0.00</span><h3>Ana opens her laptop and pulls the catalog</h3><p>Her local cache refreshes the gold tables (1.1 GB). Ten <code>lakelet sql</code> queries while she checks yesterday's numbers, all green, all local.</p></div></div>
      <div class="panel tl"><time>10:30</time><i class="g"></i><div><span class="cost">$0.00</span><h3>Ben iterates on <code>silver.charges</code></h3><p>Forty-two runs of one model against one day of bronze over two hours. Each one green, two seconds, tests included. On the old warehouse this was forty-two resumes of a Small warehouse.</p></div></div>
      <div class="panel tl"><time>11:45</time><i class="g"></i><div><span class="cost">$0.00</span><h3>Priya in finance asks a question</h3><p>"Refunds as a share of revenue by month this year." Lakelet writes the SQL against <code>gold.daily_revenue</code>, shows it, runs it locally in 0.3 s, charts it. She saves it; it becomes a gold model in a PR.</p></div></div>
      <div class="panel tl"><time>14:20</time><i class="r"></i><div><span class="cost">$0.19</span><h3>Ben's sessionisation change needs a year of events</h3><p><code>--full-refresh</code> on <code>silver.events</code>: 31 GB across 365 partitions, 22 minutes of download on his laptop or 90 seconds on an L worker. He sees the $0.30 cap, types <code>y</code>, and keeps working.</p></div></div>
      <div class="panel tl"><time>15:00</time><i class="y"></i><div><span class="cost">$0.00</span><h3>A wide join goes yellow</h3><p>Ana's ad-hoc join of events to customers wants 14 GB on a 16 GB laptop. The gauge says "runs here, slowly, ~3 min, spills to disk; burst ~40 s for $0.08". She waits the three minutes.</p></div></div>
      <div class="panel tl"><time>16:30</time><i class="g"></i><div><span class="cost">$0.11</span><h3>A coding agent answers a support ticket</h3><p>Claude Code, over MCP, calls <code>estimate</code> then <code>query</code> on gold to pull one customer's usage. Green, 1.8 s. Later it tries something on bronze; red, cap $0.30; it bursts once and stops well inside its $2.00 daily cap.</p></div></div>
      <div class="panel tl"><time>02:00</time><i class="n"></i><div><span class="cost">$0.09</span><h3>Ops job: compaction and snapshot expiry</h3><p>Small files from the day's incremental merges are rewritten into larger ones and snapshots older than 30 days are expired, on a worker, so tomorrow's manifests stay small and the gauge stays accurate.</p></div></div>
      <div class="panel tl" style="border-color:var(--local)"><time>total</time><i class="g"></i><div><span class="cost">$0.45</span><h3>The day's cloud compute</h3><p>Five people, one agent, one full refresh, one scheduled run, one ops job. Everything else was laptops. This was a busy day; most have no burst at all, and a month of this team comes to about nine bursts and a few dollars of compute on top of five Team seats.</p></div></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ================= COMPARISON ================= -->
  <section id="compare">
    <div class="eyebrow"># what changes</div>
    <h2 class="sec">The same medallion, minus the parts that only existed to keep a cluster busy.</h2>
    <p class="sub">If you've built this on Databricks or Snowflake, the layers, the dbt project and the tests are the same. These are the parts that move.</p>
    <div class="tblwrap">
      <table class="matrix">
        <tr><th></th><th>Warehouse-hosted medallion</th><th>Lakelet</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Where bronze loads run</td><td>An always-on or auto-resuming cluster; billed while awake</td><td>A scheduled worker in your bucket's region; billed for minutes, capped, then gone</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Where silver is developed</td><td>The cluster, one resume per iteration</td><td>The engineer's laptop, free, same files as production</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Where gold is read</td><td>The warehouse, through its own connectors</td><td>Anything that speaks Iceberg REST: the app, BI, Spark, notebooks, agents</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Storage</td><td>Vendor-managed, often vendor-format<small>Delta on Databricks, FDN on Snowflake, MotherDuck's own storage</small></td><td>Apache Iceberg on Parquet in your bucket<small>readable by every engine, including theirs</small></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Catalog</td><td>Unity Catalog / Snowflake's, per vendor</td><td>Iceberg REST: Lakelet's, or your Polaris, Lakekeeper, S3 Tables</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Dev / prod</td><td>Separate schemas on the same cluster; dev still bills</td><td>Dev target writes to <code>silver_ana.*</code> in the same bucket; costs nothing</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Backfills</td><td>Size up the cluster, hope, size down later</td><td>Red verdict with a price; a worker sized to that one run</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Cost visibility</td><td>Monthly invoice, per-query attribution is extra work</td><td>Every run prints estimate, actual and cap; agents and schedules carry their own caps</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Leaving</td><td>Export</td><td>Point another engine at the bucket</td></tr>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="honest">
      <h3>Where this stops being a fit</h3>
      <p><b>Streaming bronze.</b> Kafka-fed, minute-latency bronze isn't on Lakelet's roadmap. If bronze is a stream, keep the streaming layer and let Lakelet own silver and gold over what it lands.</p>
      <p><b>Terabyte-scale silver.</b> When every silver run is red, you are running a warehouse on burst. Lakelet still avoids idle time and still leaves you in Iceberg, but the free laptop tier stops doing most of the work. See <a href={url("/pricing#compare")}>where Lakelet isn't the cheapest</a>.</p>
      <p><b>Hundreds of concurrent readers on gold.</b> Publish gold to your bucket and let a serving layer or a BI tool read it directly; that's what the open catalog is for.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="cta-band" id="waitlist">
    <h2 class="sec">Bring your dbt project. Keep your layers.</h2>
    <p class="lede">Private beta on macOS and Linux. The medallion above runs on the free tier; add Team when a second laptop needs the same catalog.</p>
    <a class="btn" href={url("/#waitlist")}>Get early access</a>
  </section>

  
